<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jūnihitoe</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as itsutsuginu-karaginu-mo</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The jūnihitoe, literally twelve layers, is a style of formal court dress first worn in the Heian period by noblewomen and ladies-in-waiting at the Japanese Imperial Court. It consisted of many layered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> robes, the outer ones cut larger and thinner to show those beneath, over an innermost kosode and long red hakama, with a short coat and a train for formal occasions; early versions could weigh as much as 20 kilograms. The colour combinations of the layers, kasane no irome, were named for and evoked the seasons. It first appeared around the 10th century, and its layers were much reduced by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamakura">Kamakura</a> period. Members of the Imperial House still wear it on important occasions.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/junihitoe.jpg" alt="Jūnihitoe" width="330" height="385" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 宮内省 (Imperial Household Agency) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Empress_Nagako-1926.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
